
## Bandit Level 12 → Level 13


**Challenge:** 

The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt, which is a hexdump of a file that has been repeatedly compressed. For this level it may be useful to create a directory under /tmp in which you can work. Use mkdir with a hard to guess directory name. Or better, use the command “mktemp -d”. Then copy the datafile using cp, and rename it using mv (read the manpages!)

Commands you may need to solve this level
grep, sort, uniq, strings, base64, tr, tar, gzip, bzip2, xxd, mkdir, cp, mv, file


**Solution & Explanation:****
```
STEP 1: ls
# list files in the current directory
# data.txt should be present

STEP 2: mkdir /tmp/bandit12
# make a private working folder (safe place to create files)

STEP 3: cp data.txt /tmp/bandit12
# copy the challenge file into your temp workspace

STEP 4: cd /tmp/bandit12
# move into your workspace

STEP 5: xxd -r data.txt > data.bin
# xxd -r reverses a hexdump back into the original binary
# data.bin is now the “real” compressed file (but we don't know what type yet)

STEP 6: file data.bin
# tells you what kind of file it currently is (gzip, bzip2, tar, etc.)
# we will repeat this pattern: file -> rename -> decompress -> file again

STEP 7: mv data.bin data.gz
# rename it to match what file reports (example: gzip)
# (the extension helps you remember what tool to use)

STEP 8: gunzip data.gz
# decompress gzip
# this produces a new file (usually named data)

STEP 9: file data
# check the new file type after decompression

STEP 10: (repeat) rename + decompress based on what "file" says
# common sequence you’ll see in this level:
# gzip -> bzip2 -> gzip -> tar -> tar -> bzip2 -> tar -> gzip -> plain text

# Examples of the tools you’ll use during the loop:
# - gzip:    gunzip file.gz
# - bzip2:   bunzip2 file.bz2
# - tar:     tar -xf archive.tar

STEP 11: cat data8
# once file says "ASCII text", cat it
# the output contains the password for bandit13


```
